"src": "Marion Hudson, Brushwork: Elementary Brush-Forms, Part I (3rd ed., 1903), archive.org brushworkelement00huds. Video: Happy Handicrafts, “A Beginner’s Guide to Brushwork”, Lesson 6.",
"text": [
[
"1. Look first",
"Tell a simple tale of autumn time, introducing country lanes with hips on the hedges. Have a bunch of hips to show, and, if possible, distribute amongst the children. Let the children thoroughly examine the berries and leaves, and note the number, arrangement, colour, etc., viz.: That the berries generally go in pairs, though there may be several pairs close together on the main stalk. That the little leaflets forming the leaves are opposite, but the leaves themselves grow alternately on the stalk. That the hips have little brown horns at the top, and what they are, what once grew there, and what is inside the hip, etc."
],
[
"2. The red",
"With the red colour make the Brush-forms on the chequers from the teacher’s dictation, thus— Count the margin and four chequers from the edge of the page, and miss the margin at the top. Make one hip, with point of brush upwards. From the bottom of this hip, count two chequers towards the edge, and make one hip across, and so on. The leaves to be dictated in the same manner. It is necessary to be careful that the Brush-forms are placed on the chequers indicated, or the character of the growth will be lost. Add stalks and calyx with the same colour, using point of brush."
],
[
"3. The green",
"Free the brush as far as possible from the red colour, without washing it—take green, and make the leaves in the same manner. With the point of the brush, put green over the red of the calyx and stalks."
]
]
